# Everything here is excluded from the build context — the tarball Docker sends
# to the daemon before the first instruction runs. Two reasons it matters:
#
#   SPEED. node_modules in a pnpm monorepo is hundreds of megabytes of symlinks.
#   Shipping it makes every build slower, and it is rebuilt inside the image
#   anyway — for the correct platform, which the host copy is not.
#
#   SAFETY. `COPY . .` copies whatever is in the context. A .env in the context
#   is an API key baked into an image layer, and layers are forever: deleting
#   the file in a later instruction does NOT remove it, it is still readable
#   with `docker history`. This file is the thing standing between a secret and
#   a published image.

node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build outputs — regenerated inside the image. Copying stale ones in risks
# shipping an artifact nobody built from the source in this commit.
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# SECRETS. Never in an image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local database and its WAL/shm sidecars. Production data lives on a mounted
# volume, not in the image — an image is immutable and rebuilt constantly, so
# anything written inside it is lost on the next deploy.
data
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

.git
.github
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
*.log
coverage
scratchpad
README.md
CLAUDE.md
